A||||19||Urchin||Quad 4s||||1||
Z||000000||Urchin||07-30-2001||06:07 PM||||I was just wondering if someone could give me some info on the quad 4s.  I'm not too farmiliar with them.  Were they only available in '88?  How much faster are they than the dukes?  How rare are they?  Any tech stats?  Anything would be of interest to me.  <BR>Thanks....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lowered_blackgtthis_one.jpg"> <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000001||p4n1c||07-30-2001||06:12 PM||||The Oldsmobile Quad4 was a big step in GM 4-cyls.  It had two versions, the low output which put out about 160hp I believe and the high output version putting out 180-190hp.  I believe they were 2.5l engines, maybe 2.3l, DOHC.<P>Used in many applications, L-Bodies and W-Bodies are the most prominent in my mind.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Urchin||07-30-2001||06:22 PM||||Oldsmobile was the only company to offer them then?||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||07-30-2001||06:26 PM||||No, it was just designed by the folks at Olds.  You can find the Quad 4 in Berettas, Grand Ams, Achievas, etc.  Look at early 90's versions.....I usually see a half dozen or so at any given time in the junkyards around here, so they are fairly plentiful.  <P><BR>BTW.....they were 2.3L.  I would recommend getting a HO version.....they were rated at 180HP.  The 190HP versions are harder to find, but obviously use one if you can locate one.  I think the 190HP versions were only offered in the top-end Achievas (the SC ?), but I could be wrong.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 07-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||p4n1c||07-30-2001||06:54 PM||||Mr. Fiero:<P>Yup, I believe the Acheiva SC had the 190hp version of the Quad4.  Probably just better air flow and exhaust flow.  <P>Urchin: <P>If you can find a GTZ then it'll have the HO Quad4 in it, the Beretta's are usually plentiful.<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 07-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake2m4||07-30-2001||07:03 PM||||quad 4's were never stock in fieros if thats what you are asking.  I have one in my grand am.  Its pretty nice about 90k with no prob.  I get 25 - 30 mpg and I drive hard.<P>In grand ams from 95 - like 97, all the quads where 150 hp.  Before 95 the basic car had a 130 hp version and the GT grand am had a 180 hp High output version.  in 95 grand am GTs started to get 6 cyl engines.<P>I know it was the most common GM 4 cyl for a long while and lots of cars had them. chevy cavaliers, olds, pontiacs, etc.  I only know specifics about GA's though.<P>Jake<BR><P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jake2m4||07-30-2001||07:09 PM||||Oooh Yeah.<BR>  They are 2.3 liters at least in GAs.  I've seen turbocharger's for quad 4's that take you up from into the 200's depending on which version you have, it can be higher.  They were home made selling on the internet.<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
Z||000007||Santa Cruzer||07-30-2001||08:14 PM||||anyone have any convertions doen on this motor with pics? is it alote of modding?<P>------------------<BR>BlOw bIg cHuNkS oF aMeRiCaN v6 YoU rIcE CrApEr'S!||||reg||7
Z||000008||Standard||07-30-2001||11:32 PM||||The 190 hp quads were in Olds Achieva SCX's and (i think) W-41 442's.. More aggresive cam grind and different chip, 7400 RPM redline.<P>The best years for a Quad HO are 90-92.  Better flowing intake and exhaust manifolds, although all the HO's are rated at 180hp.  Absolutly stay away from any '95 quad 4.  It was a transitional year with balance shafts added.  Smoothed 'em out a bit, but cost a lot of horsepower.<P>Also stay away from the LO and SOHC models.. especially the SOHC, they have problems with cracked heads.  If you want good fuel economy and can get an LO with a wiring harness cheap, and can do all the work, go for it.  otherwise it's not worth the trouble.<P>The 95-up version is called the 2.4 twin cam.  Smaller bore, longer stroke.  Also not really worth swapping, unless you want to be unique.  Don't think anyones ever done one.<P>The quad 4 is the engine the 3.4 DOHC and northstar evolved from.  Actually one of the best engines GM's ever put out, IMHO.  180HP from a 4 cyl 10 years ago.<P>some pics...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/quad4_1.jpg"> <BR>quad on the cradle<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/quad_wiring.jpg"> <BR>so many wires...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/quad_exhaust.jpg"> <BR>exhaust routing (yes that's the stock exhaust manifold)<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/engine_shot_above.jpg"> <BR>The finished product.<P>All the credit for this work goes to Pherder!  He did a great job on this coupe!<P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||07-30-2001||11:40 PM||||oh yeah... basic info on the stock HO quad 4's..<P>180 HP<BR>165 Ft/lbs of torque<BR>6500 RPM redline<P>They were really restricted on the exhaust side from the factory.. good exhaust can add anywhere from 5-15 HP, maybe more.  <P>The main head gasket problem most heard about was because of an batch of improperly stamped head gaskets in the early production run..  The other head gasket problem is that there's a coolant passage really close to one of the cylinder walls.  Top-quality head gaskets are a must!  Especially if you're going to do forced induction.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Rogue_Ant||07-30-2001||11:45 PM||||Standard, just ondering any 0-60 & 1/4mi times?<P>Rogue||||reg||5
Z||000011||87FieroGTx||07-31-2001||12:41 AM|||| There is a turbo that takes the Q4's to 325hp. They rock with manual but I hear there not all that great with an auto.<P> I saw it in Kit Car Mag, I'll try to find it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Standard||07-31-2001||12:48 AM||||I just found some formulas to calculate E.T. and MPH from horsepower and weight.. assuming I can actually drive worth a damn..<P>Based on a weight of 2700 pounds(high) and a HP of 180 (probably low) I should get an E.T in the 14.4 range at around 94-95 mph.<P>Using a more correct weight of 2550 pounds and a HP of 190 it shows an E.T. of 13.83 at 98-99 mph.<P>Again, these figures are if I can drive, get a good launch, etc..  I figure High 14's, low 15's.  0-60, i dunno.  It's pretty freakin fast in a 3rd gear blast from 70 to 100mph, though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||86SE||07-31-2001||12:59 PM||||I've raced the quad HOs and they are not that great (thats what I think).  They seem to lack bottom end torque, so in the qtr mile drag races (1993 Grand Am GT 5-speed) I wasted the quad because they seem a lot slower then the v6s off the go.  Not enough torque for the HP.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://fierodude.hypermart.net/mysig.jpg"> <BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero -- 3.4L Conversion *SUSPENDED* --<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||11
Z||000014||Standard||07-31-2001||03:03 PM||||Of course the quad's don't have low-end torque.  That's not what they were designed for.  Then again, they also only have 5 ft/lbs less than a stock 2.8 v6..<P>How you launch will make or break a race with a quad.  I've raced one of my friends in his '95 3.1 lumina a few times.  If I mess up the launch (bog it) he gets me until we get up to 50-60, then i start pulling on him.  If I get a good launch, he's toast.||||reg||1
Z||000015||87GTZ34||07-31-2001||04:58 PM||||A good article in KitCar this month about the Quad build-ups and tricks.  Written by a HotRod editor with help from Oldsmobile.||||reg||1
Z||000016||loafer87gt||07-31-2001||06:50 PM||||HOw much does the Quad 4 weigh? Is it quite a bit less than the heavy 2.8?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Urchin||07-31-2001||07:05 PM||||Will it bolt up to a stock fiero trans.?||||reg||1
Z||000018||Standard||08-02-2001||02:55 PM||||Sorry for the late reply's, been gone for a bit.<P>Not sure of the exact weight, but I've heard it cuts off 100 or so pounds.<P>No, the quad 4 will not bolt up to the Fiero tranny.  The quad 4 has a unique bellhouse bolt pattern.  You have to use a quad tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000019||MoneypitGT||08-02-2001||07:12 PM||||Minus a/c it is about 150lbs lighter. It is the closest you can get to 50/50 weight distribution. It is also lighter than the PUKE.<BR>  Chris<P>------------------<BR><B>Check out my site at:</B>   <A HREF="http://www.customdynamix.com" TARGET=_blank>www.customdynamix.com</A> <BR>2 '87 GT Projects||||reg||1
